/* COMP5047 — quiz bank (study-guide + foundations style), English UI.
 * Entry shape: { ch, type: "study"|"math", q, opts: [4 options], answer: <zero-based index>, exp }
 */
var ALL_QUIZ_DATA = [
  /* Ch1 */
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "The Week 1 lecture says the reading of ubiquitous computing that matters is a claim about which of the following?", opts: ["Hardware count: computation became cheap and small enough that computers are now everywhere", "Attention: computers are embedded into everyday objects and environments until they disappear from awareness", "Speed: embedded processors became fast enough to replace the desktop machine", "Connectivity: every object is now networked, and networking is what makes computing pervasive"], answer: 1, exp: "The section's reading-that-matters is about attention, not hardware count: the markable version adds the consequence that computation moves into the environment so that attention can go back to people and tasks. The first option is the lecture's named literal reading - true as far as it goes, but it stops at the observation and says nothing about interaction." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "The Week 1 tables pair each computing era with a ratio of people to machines. Which pairing is correct?", opts: ["Mainframe - one person : many machines", "PC - many people : one machine", "Ubicomp - one person : one machine", "Ubicomp - one person : many machines"], answer: 3, exp: "The era table gives mainframe as many people to one computer, PC as one person to one computer, and ubicomp as one person to many computers living in the walls, objects and environment. The third option is the tempting mismatch because it attaches the PC ratio to the ubiquitous era, and the people-to-machine ratio is exactly what keeps the three eras distinct." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A wearable signals an incoming meeting with a slow vibration you can feel while you keep talking, with no screen and no sound. Which statement best explains why this counts as calm technology?", opts: ["Because the vibration is silent and low-powered, and calm technology is defined as technology that makes no noise", "Because the user has to interpret the vibration deliberately, which keeps the interruption under their control", "Because the information sits in the periphery of attention and can move to the centre when it becomes relevant", "Because the system acts autonomously, so the user is not involved at all"], answer: 2, exp: "Calm technology informs without demanding attention: information is encoded in the periphery, where it can be noticed without attending to it and pulled to the centre of attention when it matters, which is what a nudge you need not interpret consciously does. The first option is the page's named common wrong answer - calm is about attention, not volume, so being silent and low-powered is not what earns the label, and the fourth option confuses calm with a system that acts on its own." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A meeting room detects that people are talking, dims its lights and shows a busy panel outside. Using the Week 1 definition, which part of this is the context?", opts: ["The microphone and occupancy sensors that detected the talking", "The change in light level that the room produced in response", "The busy panel that reports the state to people outside", "The inferred situation: the room is occupied and a conversation is happening"], answer: 3, exp: "Context is information that characterises the situation of an entity and is used to adapt behaviour, so the inferred situation is the context, while the light change is the adaptation and the panel is a display. The first option is the Foundations page's named common wrong answer - confusing context with the sensor that produced it, since sensors report raw readings rather than context." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "Two colleagues' phones detect that they are near each other at lunchtime, so the system infers they are eating together and holds their calls. Which category of context is doing the work?", opts: ["Location, because nearness is being measured with proximity radios", "Social / relational, because the category answers who is with whom: co-presence, proximity and shared activity", "Identity, because the system had to recognise both people first", "Time, because the inference depends on it being lunchtime"], answer: 1, exp: "The category table lists co-presence, proximity, conversation and shared activity under social / relational context, which answers who is with whom. The first option is the tempting error because BLE beacons appear in the location row as signals, but the information actually being used here is about the relationship between people rather than about their coordinates." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A museum installs beacons so that a phone app shows information about the exhibit a visitor is standing in front of. Why is this kept closer to mobile than to pervasive computing?", opts: ["Because beacons are cheap, simple devices that do not count as pervasive hardware", "Because it uses location, and using location is a mobile-computing technique", "Because the reasoning and the screen still live on one personal general-purpose device that the visitor must attend to", "Because it uses only one category of context, and pervasive systems must use all five"], answer: 2, exp: "The classification checklist starts with where the computation lives: here one personal general-purpose device does the reasoning and the visitor must look at its screen, so the place supplies only a location cue and the attention demanded stays high. The second option is the tempting error - location is context use, and context use is what pervasive systems do, so using it does not by itself make a system pervasive." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A team reads that koalas are being hit on a particular road and builds a sensor-triggered warning sign for drivers there. On the Week 1 diagram, which process is this?", opts: ["Problem to inquiry to solution: the design follows from studying an observed real situation", "Opportunity to invention to solution, because a new artefact was created", "Innovation, because drivers would be adopting a new practice", "Neither: it only counts as design once a usability evaluation has been run"], answer: 0, exp: "The work starts from an observed situation, so it sits on the needs-driven side of the diagram, where understanding the situation produces the design and the artefact is the response to what was learned. The second option is the temptation the page warns about: creating a new artefact does not make a process invention-led, because nothing here begins from a speculative technology push." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "Which sequence matches the six design-process steps that the Week 1 tutorial implies?", opts: ["Observe a specific situation, frame a how-might-we question, ideate many responses before judging any, prototype with microcontrollers, sensors and actuators, evaluate in the context it was designed for, fold what you learned into the next iteration", "Observe a specific situation, ideate many responses, frame the how-might-we question, prototype, evaluate in context, iterate", "Frame the how-might-we question, observe a specific situation, ideate, prototype, evaluate, iterate", "Observe a specific situation, frame the how-might-we question, prototype, ideate many responses, evaluate, iterate"], answer: 0, exp: "The page's order is observe, frame the opportunity as a how-might-we question, ideate before judging, prototype, evaluate in the context it was designed for, then fold what you learned into the next iteration. The second option is the tempting slip: it keeps all six steps but ideates before the question is framed, whereas the page frames the question first so the ideation is aimed at the observed situation." },
  { ch: 1, type: "math", q: "A study room contains 12 embedded computing devices (lights, sensors, a display) and serves 4 people at a time. Using the Foundations notation D/P, what follows?", opts: ["D/P = 12 / 4 = 3, which is much greater than 1: the many-machines-per-person pattern of the ubiquitous era", "D/P = 4 / 12 = 0.33, which is much less than 1: the mainframe pattern of shared, scheduled access", "D/P = 3, which is the PC-era pattern because there is more than one device per person", "D/P cannot be formed here, because the notation counts only the devices the user carries"], answer: 0, exp: "D counts the computing devices in the environment, not those carried by the user, and P counts the people the environment serves, so D/P = 12 / 4 = 3, and the symbol table maps D/P much greater than 1 to the ubiquitous era of embedded, special-purpose, cooperating machines. The second option is the tempting slip of inverting the ratio, which puts the value below 1 and mislabels the room as mainframe, while the PC era is defined by D/P = 1." },
  { ch: 1, type: "math", q: "The Foundations page gives h_vision of about 100 years, h_applications of about 10 years and h_tech of about 1 year, and dates MEMEX to 1945. Placing MEMEX on the vision horizon, which statement is correct?", opts: ["1945 + 10 = 1955, because applications are judged on the ten-year horizon", "1945 + 100 = 2045, about 100 times the one-year technology horizon", "2045, but only about 10 times the one-year technology horizon", "No horizon applies to MEMEX, because 1945 predates ubiquitous computing in 1991"], answer: 1, exp: "MEMEX is judged as a vision, so the arithmetic is 1945 + 100 = 2045, and 100 years divided by the one-year technology horizon gives a factor of 100. The third option keeps the right year but is out by a factor of ten, while the first misuses the ten-year applications horizon, which the page reserves for products judged by whether people adopt them." },

  /* Ch2 */
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "According to the Week 2 definitions, what is an input device?", opts: ["Any component that sends data to the computer, including a keyboard shortcut defined in software", "A transducer that senses physical properties of people, places or things", "A surface that emits or modifies the physical environment so the user can perceive it", "Only a device that the user deliberately touches or holds while commanding the system"], answer: 1, exp: "The exact definition is a transducer that senses physical properties of people, places or things, which is why a camera sensing a room still counts and contact is not required. The first option is the page's named common wrong answer - anything that sends data to the computer would include a software shortcut, which senses nothing physical." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "In the deck's drawing-competition example, why is a smooth curve hard to draw on an Etch-a-Sketch but easy with a stylus on a tablet?", opts: ["Because the Etch-a-Sketch dials sense angle, while a stylus senses force", "Because a stylus supports a higher control-display gain than two dials can", "Because an Etch-a-Sketch cannot represent a path task at all", "Because the path becomes two coupled one-dimensional quantify tasks (X and Y) instead of one two-dimensional position task"], answer: 3, exp: "A stylus on a tablet senses position in two dimensions, so the whole path is one position task articulated by a single gesture, whereas the two dials split it into quantify X plus quantify Y that must be coordinated by one pair of fingers. The distractor claiming an Etch-a-Sketch cannot represent a path task is wrong because it still draws a path - a series of positions over time - it simply expresses it through coupled primitives rather than pointing." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "A stylus tablet, a touchscreen and a mouse are being classified on the axes of directness and absoluteness. Which statement is correct?", opts: ["The stylus tablet is direct and absolute, while the touchscreen is indirect and relative", "The mouse is direct but relative, because its cursor lives on the display", "The stylus tablet is absolute but indirect, the touchscreen is both absolute and direct, and the mouse is relative and indirect", "Directness and absoluteness are one axis, so any absolute device is automatically direct"], answer: 2, exp: "The page's warning is that these are different axes: the tablet senses absolute position while its display is elsewhere, the touchscreen unifies input and display in one surface, and the mouse senses relative motion away from the display. The last option is exactly the error that loses exam marks - absolute position sensing does not make a device direct." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "A stylus tablet has been used to set a value, then the value is changed in software; the pen now sits at the top-left while the indicator sits in the middle of the screen. What does this illustrate?", opts: ["The nulling problem: an absolute position-sensing device can silently disagree with the software's value", "Clutching: the pen has run out of physical space and the user must lift and re-centre it", "Quantisation: the tablet reports too few distinct positions to represent the value", "A missing transfer function: no mapping exists from pen position to screen position"], answer: 0, exp: "Because position sensing makes the device absolute, a physical intermediary can drift out of step with a value that software changed - the classic fixes are to motorise the control or to null it on the next touch. The tempting clutching answer belongs to relative motion devices that run out of desk space, and relative devices cannot have this problem at all because they only ever report change." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "A designer gives a self-centring, force-sensing isometric joystick a position-to-position transfer function. What follows?", opts: ["The joystick will begin reporting absolute screen coordinates instead of forces", "Nothing measurable, because a transfer function only changes how the device feels", "The mapping contradicts the property sensed: a self-centring force device needs force-to-velocity so the user can control speed and stop predictably", "The control-display gain simply has to be halved to compensate for the mismatch"], answer: 2, exp: "The property sensed dictates the mapping class - position-to-position for absolute position devices, velocity-to-velocity for relative motion devices, and force-to-velocity rate control for isometric self-centring devices. The nothing-measurable distractor fails because transfer functions are not cosmetic: with no movement to measure and a self-centring rest position, a position mapping leaves no usable control of speed." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "Using the deck's stylus states, order the events from the very first condition to the end of a stroke.", opts: ["Tip touches (tracking), dragging, lift stylus, tip releases, hover, out of range", "Out of range, hover, dragging, tip touches (tracking), lift stylus, tip releases", "Hover, out of range, tip touches (tracking), dragging, tip releases, lift stylus", "Out of range, hover, tip touches (tracking), dragging, lift stylus, tip releases"], answer: 3, exp: "The tablet senses nothing first, then reports position while the tip is close (hover), then contact begins tracking, then the tip stays down while it moves (dragging), and lift and release are separate transitions - which is what makes hover previews possible. The ordering that puts dragging before the tip touches is impossible, because dragging is defined by the tip being down, so contact has to begin first." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "A meeting room raises its blinds when a light sensor reads above 400 lx, whether or not anyone is in the room. Where does this sit on the intentionality spectrum?", opts: ["Intentional, because somebody must have deliberately let the light into the room", "Expected, because the room's occupants anticipate that the blinds will open", "Incidental, because the system acts on a phenomenon that nobody aimed at it", "It is not interaction at all, because no user issues a command to the system"], answer: 2, exp: "Incidental is the end of the spectrum where the system reads a phenomenon nobody directed at it, so the design question becomes what happens when that inference is wrong and who notices. The expected option is the tempting one, but expected requires an action the user actually carries out and anticipates - walking in knowing the lights will come on - and here there is no user action at all." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "Between strokes, users naturally rest their hand on the table, and the sensor reports nothing at all while they do. Which expected / sensed / desired relationship is this, and what is the standard design response?", opts: ["Sensed but not expected - treat the stillness as a sensor fault and raise a warning", "Expected but not sensed - keep it unsensed and treat it as a rest space and a moment of physical preparation", "Desired but neither expected nor sensed - change the application so that the pause becomes the input", "Expected and sensed - map the pause to an explicit command the user can repeat"], answer: 1, exp: "The movement is natural (expected) but the sensors miss it, and the documented response for that region is to exploit the gap as a rest space, physical preparation, follow-through and expressive latitude rather than dead space. The desired-but-neither option is wrong because that region describes something the application wants that users would not naturally do, whereas here the user performs the pause happily and only the sensing is missing." },
  { ch: 2, type: "math", q: "On the foundations page, a toolbar button shrinks from 40 px to 20 px wide while the pointer travels A = 360 px to reach it. Using ID = log2(A/W + 1), what happens to the index of difficulty?", opts: ["It rises from log2(360/40 + 1) = log2 10, about 3.32 bits, to log2(360/20 + 1) = log2 19, about 4.25 bits - an increase of roughly 0.93 bits, or 28%", "It rises from 9 bits to 19 bits, because A/W + 1 grows from 10 to 19 when the width halves", "It rises from 3.32 bits to 4.32 bits, the value Hick's law gives for a menu of 19 alternatives", "It falls from 4.25 bits to 3.32 bits, because a narrower button is quicker to hit accurately"], answer: 0, exp: "Before: 360/40 + 1 = 10, so ID = log2 10 is about 3.32 bits; after: 360/20 + 1 = 19, so ID = log2 19 is about 4.25 bits, a growth of roughly 0.93 bits (about 28%), and the extra time is b times 0.93 where b is the fitted slope for that device. The 4.32 bit option is the most tempting distractor because that number really is on the page, but it is Hick's H = log2(19 + 1) for a 19-item menu, not this button's index of difficulty." },
  { ch: 2, type: "math", q: "A toolbar becomes a menu of n = 19 equally probable items. Using Hick's law H = log2(n + 1), what is the decision entropy, and why is the value interesting?", opts: ["log2(19), about 4.25 bits - identical to the narrow button's Fitts difficulty, so the two laws are unrelated", "log2(19 + 1) = log2 20, about 4.32 bits, almost exactly the Fitts index of difficulty of the 20 px button, which is why the reading suspects the two laws are one law", "19 bits, one bit per alternative in the menu", "log2(600/30 + 1), about 4.39 bits, taken from the far-and-small target in the difficulty chart"], answer: 1, exp: "Hick's law adds one before taking the logarithm: H = log2(19 + 1) = log2 20 is about 4.32 bits for 19 equally probable alternatives, and the near-coincidence with the 20 px button's 4.25 bits is the reading's point. The tempting log2(19) answer drops the + 1 and merely reproduces the Fitts index of difficulty of the narrow button; the 4.39 bit option is a real chart value but it belongs to the A = 600, W = 30 target." },

  /* Ch3 */
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A digital input pin on a 5 V board is measured at 2.4 V: above the input LOW threshold (at most 1.5 V) but below the input HIGH threshold (at least 3.0 V). What does the firmware see?", opts: ["HIGH, because 2.4 V is closer to 5 V than to 0 V", "An undefined level: the reading is not guaranteed either way, because 2.4 V sits inside the band between the two thresholds", "The value 2.4, because a digital read reports the measured voltage", "LOW, because nothing is actively driving the pin up to 5 V"], answer: 1, exp: "2.4 V lies inside the undefined band between the input LOW threshold (at most 1.5 V) and the input HIGH threshold (at least 3.0 V), so the state is not guaranteed - the classic floating-input symptom that a pull-up or pull-down resistor fixes. The tempting option about returning the value 2.4 describes an analogue input: a digital receiver applies thresholds and digitalRead() returns a state, never a voltage." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "Which statement defines GPIO (general-purpose input/output) as this chapter uses the term?", opts: ["A pin whose voltage is fixed at the logic supply by the board, so the circuit decides what it does", "A pin that the program can only read and never drive", "A pin whose direction and behaviour are chosen by the program with pinMode(), rather than fixed by the circuit", "A pin that supplies power to other components, like a small battery"], answer: 2, exp: "GPIO stands for general-purpose input/output, and the general purpose is exactly the point: the program, not the circuit, assigns the pin's direction with pinMode() before the pin behaves predictably. The tempting first option is the common wrong answer named on the foundations page - treating a pin as fixed-purpose, like a resistor or an LED." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "Which row correctly contrasts the Arduino Uno with the FireBeetle 2 ESP32-E as the study guide describes them?", opts: ["Uno: 5 V logic and 14 digital I/O pins, six of them PWM-capable; ESP32: 3.3 V logic and 18 digital I/O", "Uno: 3.3 V logic and built-in WiFi; ESP32: 5 V logic and no wireless", "Uno: 240 MHz clock and USB-C power; ESP32: 16 MHz clock and a 7-12 V barrel jack", "Uno: 11 analogue inputs and included in Tinkercad; ESP32: 6 analogue inputs and also included in Tinkercad"], answer: 0, exp: "The Uno is the 5 V, 16 MHz board with 14 digital pins (six PWM-capable), 6 analogue inputs (A0-A5) and USB-B or barrel-jack power, while the ESP32 board is 3.3 V, 240 MHz, with 18 digital I/O, 11 analogue inputs and built-in WiFi and Bluetooth. The clock-and-power row is tempting because it mixes real features, but it gives each board the other's clock speed and power connector; the analogue-input row reverses both counts - the Uno has 6 channels (A0-A5) and the ESP32 has 11 - and claims Tinkercad support for both boards, when only the Uno is included." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A student is building this chapter's button-and-LED circuit for the first time. Which ordering matches the recommended build-and-test procedure?", opts: ["Upload the sketch first, then choose the resistor once you can see how bright the LED is", "Wire the button and enable INPUT_PULLUP first, then size the LED's resistor, then place the LED", "Place the LED and resistor on the breadboard, then read the LED datasheet, then choose the resistor value", "Read the LED datasheet, compute R = (V_supply - V_f)/I, place the LED and resistor respecting polarity and wire the branch, then wire the button to GND with INPUT_PULLUP, verify the raw readings, and finally add the debounce window"], answer: 3, exp: "The procedure settles the electricity before the logic: read the datasheet's forward voltage and rated current, choose the resistor, place the parts with the correct polarity, wire the branch to a pin and the ground rail, wire the button to GND with INPUT_PULLUP, verify the raw readings in the Serial Monitor, then add the debounce window. The tempting distractor places the LED and resistor on the breadboard before consulting the datasheet: placement feels harmless, but it puts parts on the board before the datasheet has said which resistor the LED needs and which way round its legs go." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A sketch calls pinMode(7, INPUT) and nothing at all is wired to pin 7. What does digitalRead(7) return?", opts: ["Always LOW, because nothing is driving the pin high", "Exactly 2.5, the midpoint of the 5 V supply, because the pin is balanced", "Unpredictable values that drift from moment to moment, because the pin is floating", "Always HIGH, because INPUT switches on the chip's internal pull-up"], answer: 2, exp: "A plain INPUT pin is high-impedance, so with nothing connected it behaves like a small antenna and its voltage drifts inside the undefined band, giving readings that change from moment to moment. Always LOW is the common wrong answer listed on the foundations page - a floating input reads noise, which is worse than a defined level - and the internal pull-up comes from INPUT_PULLUP, not INPUT." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "An LED stays dark even though its series resistor is the correct value and the pin is driven HIGH. Inspection shows the longer leg is connected towards the ground rail and the shorter leg towards the pin. What is wrong?", opts: ["The LED is reverse-biased: it is a diode, so current flows only from the anode (longer leg) to the cathode (shorter leg, the flat side of the package)", "The LED has no forward voltage drop, so the resistor is dropping the whole 5 V", "The series resistor is unnecessary here and is blocking the current, so removing it would light the LED", "Digital pins cannot drive LEDs at all, so an external transistor is always required"], answer: 0, exp: "An LED is a diode, so it conducts only from the anode (the longer leg) to the cathode (the shorter leg, on the flat side), and reversing it leaves it reverse-biased and dark no matter how good the resistor value is. The tempting remove-the-resistor option is the dangerous misconception: an LED has no meaningful internal resistance, so with the resistor out of the loop the current is limited only by the wiring and its 20 mA continuous rating is exceeded immediately." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A button is wired between pin 4 and GND, and pinMode(4, INPUT_PULLUP) has been called. Which condition means the button is being held down, and why?", opts: ["digitalRead(4) == HIGH, because the pull-up holds the idle level HIGH and the press is what changes it", "digitalRead(4) == LOW, because pressing connects the pin to GND, so the wiring is active-low and the sketch must invert the logic", "The reading sits somewhere between HIGH and LOW, because the 10 kohm resistor divides the supply", "digitalRead(4) == HIGH, because the internal pull-up only switches on while the button is held"], answer: 1, exp: "With a pull-up the internal resistor holds the pin at 5 V while the switch is open, and pressing connects the pin to ground, so the reading becomes LOW and the sketch must invert the condition, which is what the deck means by 'we must invert the behaviour'. The tempting HIGH answer is the mirror-image pull-down convention, where the resistor goes to GND, the idle level is LOW and a press reads HIGH; here the resistor goes to the supply rail, so a press drags the pin down to LOW instead." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A sketch uses a 500 ms debounce window on a button that users tap quickly, for only 80-200 ms at a time. What is the likely complaint?", opts: ["The counter still increments several times per press", "The pin floats for the duration of the window", "The LED stays lit after the button is released", "Some quick presses do nothing at all, because the window outlasts the whole press"], answer: 3, exp: "A 500 ms window is longer than the 80-200 ms a person actually holds a button, so a press that begins and ends inside the window is never accepted and the button feels broken or intermittent - the inequality t_debounce much less than T_press has been violated. The counter-runs-away option is the opposite failure mode: that is what a window that is too short produces, because bounce leaks through as extra events." },
  { ch: 3, type: "math", q: "A green LED has V_f = 2.2 V and you want 12 mA through it from a 5 V pin. The exact calculation gives 233 ohm. Which resistor should you fit, and what current results?", opts: ["233 ohm, the exact result, because a resistor should match the calculation exactly", "220 ohm, the next standard value below, giving about 12.7 mA", "240 ohm, the next standard value above, giving about 11.7 mA", "The value hardly matters: any resistor of a few hundred ohms will pass exactly 12 mA"], answer: 2, exp: "R_series = (V_CC - V_f)/I_f = (5.0 - 2.2)/0.012 = 233 ohm, and the rule is to round the resistance up, because a smaller resistance means a larger current; 240 ohm gives 2.8/240 = about 11.7 mA, just under target and inside every rating. The tempting 220 ohm is the next standard value down and would give about 12.7 mA, spending the LED's safety margin in the wrong direction." },
  { ch: 3, type: "math", q: "A 5 V pin drives an LED with V_f = 2.0 V through a 330 ohm resistor, so the resistor drops 3.0 V and carries I = 3.0/330 = 9.1 mA. Which statement about the limits is correct?", opts: ["The resistor dissipates P = 3.0 x 0.0091 = about 27 mW, roughly 11 percent of a 1/4 W (250 mW) rating, while 9.1 mA is inside the pin's 20 mA recommended limit, so the pin current is the binding constraint rather than the resistor's power", "The resistor dissipates about 27 W, which exceeds the 1/4 W rating, so a higher-power part is required", "The pin is over its 20 mA recommended limit and close to the 40 mA absolute maximum, so the design is unsafe", "Both limits are exceeded at once: the resistor's power rating and the pin's current"], answer: 0, exp: "P = V x I = 3.0 x 0.0091 = about 27 mW, roughly 11 percent of the 250 mW kit resistor's rating, and the pin sources 9.1 mA, comfortably under its 20 mA recommended figure. The tempting option about exceeding the pin limit misreads the figure: it is at 30 mA - the 100 ohm case - that the GPIO becomes the binding constraint, while even then the resistor's 90 mW stays well inside its rating." },

  /* Ch4 */
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "The Week 4 guide insists on four definitions. Which term is the general name for a device that converts one form of energy into another, with sensors and actuators as its input and output cases?", opts: ["Sensor - light, force or heat in, an electrical property out", "Actuator - electrical energy out, as light, sound, motion or heat in the world", "Quantiser - a device that rounds each measurement to the nearest of a finite set of levels", "Transducer - the general case, of which a sensor is the input transducer and an actuator the output transducer"], answer: 3, exp: "The guide defines a transducer as any device that converts one form of energy into another, then names the sensor as the input transducer and the actuator as the output transducer. 'Sensor' is the tempting choice because almost every analogue input in this unit is one, but it names only the input case and would wrongly exclude an LED, speaker or motor." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A traffic-light sketch waits out its 2 s red phase inside delay(2000). A pedestrian presses the crossing button 0.4 s into that phase. What happens, and why?", opts: ["The press is stored in a buffer and handled as soon as delay() returns", "The press is lost, because delay() suspends loop() and the pin is not sampled until the delay returns", "The processor wakes on the pin change and handles the press immediately, in the middle of the delay", "The press is counted, but the light only responds at the end of the full cycle"], answer: 1, exp: "delay() does nothing but wait, so digitalRead() is never called during the 2 s phase and there is no later moment at which the press could be noticed. The 'buffer' option is the named common wrong answer: nothing is buffered, because the pin is simply not sampled while loop() is suspended, and interrupts are a different mechanism entirely." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "The guide's software debounce is a non-blocking state machine. Which sequence gives its steps in the correct order?", opts: ["Read the raw pin every pass, compare it with the last level seen, and if it changed record the time with millis(); wait until the new level has persisted for the debounce interval; discard it as bounce if it changes back sooner; once it survives, accept it as the new stable level and act on the accepted edge", "Read the raw pin, count the press immediately, wait 20 ms, then check whether the level is still the same and withdraw the count if it is not", "Read the pin, call delay(DEBOUNCE_MS), then read the pin again and compare the two readings before acting", "Timestamp every edge with millis(), accept each edge as a press, and average the press count over the debounce interval"], answer: 0, exp: "The state machine samples the pin continuously, restarts a timestamp on every raw change, and promotes the new level to 'stable' - and acts - only once it has survived the interval, so bounce is discarded rather than counted. Option 1 is the tempting one because it does check stability, but it acts on the press first and withdraws it later, which is exactly how one physical press becomes three or four counts; option 2 is the blocking variant the guide rates as acceptable only in a sketch that does nothing else." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "Which statement correctly contrasts what the two analogue functions give you on an Arduino Uno?", opts: ["analogRead() returns a voltage in volts, while analogWrite() returns the duty cycle as a fraction", "analogRead() returns 0-255 from the PWM pins, while analogWrite() accepts 0-1023 from the ADC", "analogRead() returns an integer code 0-1023 measured against a reference voltage, while analogWrite() accepts an integer 0-255 that sets a duty cycle", "Both work directly in physical units, so no conversion arithmetic and no calibration are needed"], answer: 2, exp: "analogRead() reports a code - the volts are your arithmetic, k x V_REF/2^N - and analogWrite() takes an 8-bit value that sets a duty cycle rather than a voltage, because the ATmega328P has no DAC. Option 0 is the guide's named common wrong answer ('it returns volts'), and option 1 simply swaps the two ranges." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A student writes analogWrite(7, 128), but pin 7 is an ordinary digital pin rather than one of the tilde pins. What does the Arduino core do?", opts: ["It outputs a 2.5 V average anyway, because analogWrite() works on every pin", "It falls back to a plain digital write, so a value of 128 or more simply switches the pin HIGH", "The sketch fails to compile, because pin 7 is not in the PWM table", "It interprets 128 as a frequency and toggles pin 7 at about 128 Hz"], answer: 1, exp: "The guide's 'two traps' callout states that analogWrite() only works on the PWM pins (~3, ~5, ~6, ~9, ~10, ~11) and on any other pin the core falls back to a digital write, where a value of 128 or more reads as HIGH. The 2.5 V option is tempting because v/255 x 5 V is the right arithmetic for a PWM pin, but pin 7 has no timer routed to it, so no duty cycle is produced at all." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "An LDR sits between 5 V and the output node, and a fixed 10 kOhm resistor sits between the output node and ground. The room gets brighter and the LDR's resistance falls. What does analogRead() do?", opts: ["The reading falls towards 0, because less resistance means less voltage at the node", "The reading holds near 512, because a divider pins the output to the midpoint", "The reading rises towards 1023, since V_out = 5 V x R_fixed/(R_LDR + R_fixed) and the top resistance is falling", "The reading saturates at 1023 and then stays there at every light level"], answer: 2, exp: "With the sensor as the top resistor the junction voltage is V_in x R_fixed/(R_LDR + R_fixed), so a falling R_LDR raises the output towards 5 V and the code climbs towards 1023. The 'falls towards 0' option is the classic error - that is what happens when the sensor is the bottom resistor, not the top one." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A flex sensor reads about 300 when flat and 620 when fully bent, but the sketch maps it with map(raw, 0, 1023, 0, 180). What is the consequence?", opts: ["Nothing is wrong: 0-1023 is the ADC's range, so mapping that range is always correct", "The mapping is inverted, so bending the strip decreases the reported angle", "Degrees cannot come from an integer reading, so a float formula is required instead", "The output only sweeps roughly 53 to 109 degrees, throwing away most of the 0-180 degree range and the sensor's usable resolution"], answer: 3, exp: "The sensor occupies only 300-620 of the 0-1023 range, so map() gives about 300/1023 x 180 = 53 degrees flat and 620/1023 x 180 = 109 degrees bent, compressing the useful swing into a fraction of the output range. The tempting 'nothing is wrong' option confuses the ADC's full range with the sensor's actual span, which is precisely what calibration exists to fix." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A sketch prints four values on every pass of loop() at 9600 baud with no gating. What does the guide say this costs?", opts: ["Printing is slow - a few lines per pass can stretch loop() from microseconds to milliseconds, so the printing should be gated with the millis() pattern", "Nothing: the UART shifts the characters out in hardware, so loop() never waits for the monitor", "It consumes the analogue pins A0-A5, so the sensor inputs stop working while the monitor is open", "It forces the baud rate down to 300, or the monitor shows a wall of garbage characters"], answer: 0, exp: "The guide lists printing as slow enough to turn a microsecond loop into a millisecond one and says to gate it with the millis() pattern. Option 1 is the tempting answer because the shift register really is hardware, but the sketch still blocks once the transmit buffer fills - and it is pins 0 and 1, the UART, that must stay free, not A0-A5; a baud mismatch does cause garbage, but it does not change the rate." },
  { ch: 4, type: "math", q: "A 10-bit ADC with V_REF = 5 V sees 3.00 V on its input. Using the ideal quantiser of the foundations page, which code does it return, and what voltage does one code represent?", opts: ["Code 153 - one code is 5 V/256 = 19.5 mV, the usual 8-bit step", "Code 614 - one code (one LSB) is 5 V/1024 = 4.883 mV, and floor(3.00/0.004883) = floor(614.4)", "Code 600 - one code is exactly 5 mV, because 3 V spans 600 equal steps", "Code 1023 - any input above half of V_REF saturates the converter"], answer: 1, exp: "The step size is q = V_REF/2^N = 5/1024 = 4.883 mV, so the code is k = min(2^N - 1, floor(V_in/q)) = floor(3.00/0.004883) = 614, the page's own worked number. Option 0 is the tempting one because it divides by 256, mixing the 8-bit PWM output scale into the 10-bit input converter, and 153 is a fraction of 255 rather than a legitimate code for 3 V." },
  { ch: 4, type: "math", q: "On a 5 V board a sketch calls analogWrite(LED, 102) on a PWM pin running at about 490 Hz. What are the duty cycle, the average voltage and the PWM period?", opts: ["D = 0.4, V_avg = 2.0 V, and a period of about 490 ms", "D = 0.1 and V_avg = 0.5 V, because the duty cycle is 102/1024", "D = 0.4, V_avg = 2.0 V, and a period of about 2.04 ms", "D = 0.4 and V_avg = 5 V, because the pin still switches the full supply voltage"], answer: 2, exp: "The sketch value sets D = v/255 = 102/255 = 0.4, so V_avg = D x V_CC = 0.4 x 5 = 2.0 V, and T = 1/f_PWM = 1/490 = 2.04 ms. Option 1 is the tempting error the foundations page warns about - 1024 is the input converter's code count, not the output scale - while option 3 forgets that the pin sits at 5 V for only 40 % of each period, so it is the average and not the instantaneous voltage that is 2.0 V." },

  /* Ch5 */
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "According to the Week 5 material, which statement is the definition of digital fabrication?", opts: ["Designing an object in a 3D modelling program and exporting it as a mesh", "Making a part by depositing material layer by layer", "A manufacturing process in which the machine is controlled by a computer", "Cutting a shape by hand from an outline that was drawn on a computer"], answer: 2, exp: "The definition that earns the mark requires a computer-controlled machine whose instructions come from a digital model, which is why laser cutters, vinyl cutters, water jets and CNC mills sit alongside printers. The layer-by-layer option is the named common wrong answer: it describes only the additive family, while the same week teaches subtractive and forming processes too." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "A panel was drawn in Illustrator and sent to the laser cutter, but the job came back with the engraved label only and no cut outline at all. What is the most likely cause?", opts: ["The cutting outline is not a 0.01 pt single closed path, so it would not appear when you press Ctrl+Y", "The document colour mode was left as CMYK instead of RGB", "The panel was nested outside the 600 x 900 mm bed", "The 3 mm acrylic was above the machine's 3 cm thickness limit"], answer: 0, exp: "The file-prep rule is that all shapes must be 0.01 pt single-path outlines and that Ctrl+Y reveals what will actually cut, so a path that is not a single closed outline is simply not followed by the cutter. The colour-mode distractor is tempting because colour really does map to operations, but the deck requires RGB, so CMYK is the wrong mode here and would change which pass runs rather than making the whole outline vanish; 3 mm is also far below the 3 cm thickness limit." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "One enclosure shell has to show fine detail with no visible layer lines, and only a single copy is needed. Comparing the three printer families in the Week 5 table, which process fits and why?", opts: ["FDM, because it has the cheapest cost index of the three", "SLS, because it needs no support structures", "FDM, because its 0.3-0.1 mm layers are the finest available", "SLA, because its 0.025-0.01 mm layers are the finest of the three and it suits one-off detailed parts"], answer: 3, exp: "SLA's layer height is an order of magnitude finer than FDM's and its photosensitive resins take painting or dyeing, so it is the route to fine detail on a single part at a $$ cost index. The 'SLS needs no supports' distractor is true and tempting, but SLS is the $$$ option and its 0.1-0.01 mm range is not inherently finer than SLA, so it does not answer the layer-line question." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "Which sequence reproduces the resin casting workflow given in the Week 5 deck?", opts: ["Make the silicone mould, then make the master, then add a vent and fill port, then the mould box, then fill with resin and cure, then post-processing", "Make a master (printed, then sanded and polished), make the mould box, add a vent and a fill port, make the silicone mould, fill with resin and cure, then post-processing", "Add a vent and fill port, fill with resin and cure, make the master, make the mould box, make the silicone mould, then post-processing", "Make the master, fill with resin and cure, make the silicone mould, make the mould box, add the vent and fill port, then post-processing"], answer: 1, exp: "The six steps run master, mould box, vent and fill port, silicone mould, resin and cure, post-processing, because the silicone is cast around the finished master and the vent must exist before the mould is poured. Reversing the first two steps is the tempting error, but a silicone mould cannot be made before there is a master to cast it from." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "A team needs 40 identical 8 mm acrylic side panels, each with mounting holes and an engraved label. Which process does the Week 5 material point to?", opts: ["CNC router, because a subtractive machine is the only way to cut flat sheet", "Water jet, because water never damages plastic", "Laser cutter, which the deck lists as suited to acrylic and which cuts and engraves in the same job", "SLS printing, because it needs no support structures"], answer: 2, exp: "The deck names plywood, acrylic and cardboard as laser materials, and the laser both cuts and engraves from one file, which is exactly what repeated labelled panels need. The CNC router distractor is plausible because both are subtractive, but the laser is faster on flat sheet, needs no fixturing, and the deck's own caveats for water jets (water-resistant material, heat warping) point away from that route." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "You need to know whether people can tell that the LUAU lamp is charging while it sits on its base. Which prototype answers that question most cheaply?", opts: ["A full works-like prototype with working electronics and the rotating dimming mechanism", "A cheap low-quality stand-in for the real lamp, kept rough until the production version is built", "A CAD model rendered in the modelling software", "A full-scale looks-like model in foam core or XPS that sits on the charger the same way"], answer: 3, exp: "Placement on the charger is listed as a looks-like property, so the cheapest full-scale looks-like model is the right instrument and costs an afternoon. The 'low-quality stand-in' distractor is the misconception the page names: a looks-like prototype is a different instrument with its own questions, not a poor copy of the finished product, and a rendered CAD model cannot be placed on a charger and viewed across a room." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "In the CAD to CAM pipeline, what exactly is G-code?", opts: ["The mesh file format exported from CAD, preferred in binary form over ASCII", "The machine-specific instruction stream that slicing produces and the machine executes", "The list of design-history features stored inside the CAD file", "The colour and layer convention that maps an Illustrator file to laser operations"], answer: 1, exp: "G-code is the output of the CAM stage: the model is checked, sliced into horizontal layers and emitted as machine instructions carrying the parameters that decide strength, finish and print time. The mesh distractor is tempting because .stl, .obj, .dae, .x3d, .wrl and .ply really are the handover files, but they are the input to slicing, not its output." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "A team casts 20 copies from a silicone mould and finds the same sanding marks and layer lines on every single part. What does the Week 5 moulding material say they should have done?", opts: ["Sanded and polished the master until it was better than the part they wanted, because all problems in the master are transferred to the moulded parts", "Added a second vent, because trapped air leaves identical marks on every casting", "Changed the resin-to-hardener ratio so that the castings cured harder", "Used an injection mould instead, because silicone copies surface defects"], answer: 0, exp: "The section's key sentence is that all problems in the master are transferred to the moulded parts, so layer lines and sanding marks have to be removed before the mould is made. The vent distractor is tempting because vents really do matter, but a missing vent traps bubbles; it does not copy the master's sanding marks into every part." },
  { ch: 5, type: "math", q: "A board measures 68.6 mm x 53.4 mm. Following the tutorial's tolerance of 1.00 mm per side and a 2 mm wall, what is the outside footprint of the enclosure?", opts: ["70.6 mm x 55.4 mm", "72.6 mm x 57.4 mm", "78.6 mm x 63.4 mm", "74.6 mm x 59.4 mm"], answer: 3, exp: "Inside = board + 2 x tolerance: 68.6 + 2(1.00) = 70.6 mm and 53.4 + 2(1.00) = 55.4 mm; outside = inside + 2t: 70.6 + 4 = 74.6 mm and 55.4 + 4 = 59.4 mm. The 70.6 x 55.4 distractor is the inside dimension, the answer you get if you add the tolerance but forget the wall on both sides." },
  { ch: 5, type: "math", q: "Nominal 3 mm acrylic measures 2.85 mm, the laser kerf measures 0.2 mm, and you want 0.1 mm of clearance for a hand-tight finger joint. What slot width do you draw in the file?", opts: ["3.15 mm", "2.95 mm", "2.75 mm", "2.65 mm"], answer: 2, exp: "w_slot = t_meas - k + c = 2.85 - 0.2 + 0.1 = 2.75 mm, and the beam then removes its 0.2 mm kerf so the finished slot is 2.95 mm, which is 0.1 mm looser than the sheet. The 2.95 mm distractor is the finished slot rather than the drawn one, and 3.15 mm is what you get by adding the kerf instead of subtracting it." },

  /* Ch6 */
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "Which statement gives the chapter's working definition of gestural interaction rather than a common misconception?", opts: ["Any input that uses a touchscreen, since touch is the most direct form of gesture", "Input in which the system infers intent from the movement, configuration or contact pattern of the hand and body rather than from the actuation of a dedicated control", "Any gesture that carries meaning for the user and is performed with a stylised hand shape", "Input that requires cameras in the environment so that the whole body can be tracked in mid-air"], answer: 1, exp: "Correct: gestural interaction is defined by the system inferring intent from the hand and body's movement, configuration or contact pattern, and it splits into surface gestures and mid-air gestures. Option 0 is the foundations page's own named common wrong answer, because 'any input that uses a touchscreen' names a surface rather than the defining property, and actuating a dedicated control is explicitly excluded. Option 2 fails the same way the semaphoric entry does: in this chapter every gesture type means something to someone." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "Which sequence puts the four milestones of the surface lineage in the correct chronological order?", opts: ["Sensor Frame (1963) then SketchPad (1986) then Digital Desk (1991) then multi-touch iPhone (2007)", "SketchPad (1963) then Digital Desk (1986) then Sensor Frame (1991) then multi-touch iPhone (2007)", "SketchPad (1963) then Sensor Frame (1986) then Digital Desk (1991) then multi-touch iPhone (2007)", "SketchPad (1963) then Sensor Frame (1986) then multi-touch iPhone (1991) then Digital Desk (2007)"], answer: 2, exp: "Correct: Sutherland's SketchPad is 1963, McAvinney's Sensor Frame is 1986, Wellner's Digital Desk is 1991 and capacitive multi-touch ships with the iPhone in 2007. Option 3 is the tempting distractor because it keeps the first two milestones right and only reverses the last two, but the Digital Desk precedes consumer multi-touch and each milestone adds exactly one capability the previous system lacked." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "What does the Sensor Frame (McAvinney, 1986) contribute to the lineage that SketchPad (1963) does not already demonstrate?", opts: ["The ability to draw directly on a display with a pointing device", "A set of simultaneous contact points instead of a single pointer position", "The overlaying of paper on a desk with projected digital content", "A platform-level gesture vocabulary of tap, pinch, swipe and flick"], answer: 1, exp: "Correct: the Sensor Frame reports where the hand is and how many fingers are involved, so touch stops being one cursor position and becomes a set of contact points the system must interpret. Option 0 is the tempting wrong answer, since SketchPad already draws on a display from a light pen, so rendering is not the Sensor Frame's contribution; the paper-and-projection overlay is the Digital Desk (1991) and the platform vocabulary arrives with multi-touch (2007)." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A tablet drawing app lays down a long stroke across the canvas every time the user's resting hand shifts. Which challenge of direct input is this, and which fix does the study guide give for it?", opts: ["Occlusion, fixed by displacing the visual feedback above the finger", "Parallax, fixed by calibrating the sensing layer against the display layer", "Soft versus hard touch, fixed by adding pressure or contact-area sensing", "Palm rejection, fixed by classifying contacts by size and shape and ignoring the largest blob"], answer: 3, exp: "Correct: a resting heel, thumb base or forearm is a large contact that the sensor reports as a touch, and rejecting it by size, shape or context is palm rejection. Option 1 is the most tempting distractor because parallax is also a geometric error, but it is a fixed offset between the sensing and display planes that would misplace every stroke slightly rather than add unintended ones; occlusion hides content from the eye instead of creating input." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A user swipes two fingers upwards to dismiss a notification. Which taxonomy type is this, and what must the recogniser output?", opts: ["Deictic; the recogniser must recover the screen coordinate that is being indicated", "Manipulative; the recogniser must output the continuous position of the fingers", "Semaphoric; the recogniser must output which symbol of the dictionary was performed", "Motion correlation; the recogniser must output which moving target the user matched"], answer: 2, exp: "Correct: a two-finger upward swipe is a stylised dynamic pattern drawn from a dictionary, so the output is a label rather than a target or a parameter. Option 1 tempts because the fingers do move continuously, but manipulative gestures need a tight mapping between hand movement and an entity being changed, whereas dismiss is discrete and arbitrary; a deictic reading would require the output to be a target." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A researcher must detect finger movements of a few millimetres while the participant walks around a city block. Which sensing approach fits, and why?", opts: ["Intrinsic inertial sensing, because the sensors travel with the body and resolve motion at the source", "Extrinsic structured light, because it is the most accurate option for small movements", "RGB pose estimation with OpenPose, because it needs no markers and can follow the person", "Extrinsic active IR markers, because an absolute reference never drifts and covers the whole city"], answer: 0, exp: "Correct: intrinsic sensing is attached to the body, so it leaves the room with the participant and can resolve millimetre motion at the source, which is exactly what makes microgestures practical, with drift as the accepted cost. Option 1 is the tempting distractor because structured light is precise, but it is extrinsic: the participant leaves its capture volume within a few steps, and millimetre finger detail needs a very close and stable view." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A recogniser returns 'swipe left, confidence 0.42' and the system performs the swipe anyway. Which part of the pipeline is at fault?", opts: ["Segmentation, because the gesture boundaries must be wrong", "The decision made after classification, because a confidence of 0.42 should have produced a reject or a confirmation rather than an action", "Feedback, because the user was not told that the gesture had been recognised", "Features, because a swipe and a wave cannot be separated by any feature vector"], answer: 1, exp: "Correct: classification did its job by returning a label together with a confidence, and the fault lies in the decision layer ignoring that confidence, so the system acted on its least reliable judgement. Option 0 is tempting because bad segmentation also produces bad actions, but here the gesture was already segmented and labelled; feedback happens after the action, so it cannot be the cause of an action that was taken." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "An elicitation study finds that the command 'next track' produced eleven different gestures from twenty participants. What is the correct reading of that result?", opts: ["The study failed and should be re-run with clearer instructions", "The eleven gestures should be averaged into one composite gesture", "The command has no intuitive gesture, so it should be given a visible control or an explicitly taught shortcut", "The command is intuitive but hard to sense, so the tracking hardware should be changed"], answer: 2, exp: "Correct: low agreement is a result rather than a failure, and it is evidence that the command has no intuitive gesture, so the honest design response is a visible affordance. Option 1 is the tempting distractor because pooling the proposals sounds like averaging, but the method reads the distribution of answers and a mean of eleven different gestures would be a gesture nobody proposed; agreement also says nothing about whether a sensor can see the gesture." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "An IMU has a constant accelerometer bias b = 0.01 m/s2. Using the foundations page's error model, velocity error approx b times t and position error approx one half b t squared, what are the velocity and position errors after 20 s?", opts: ["0.2 m/s and 4 m", "0.2 m/s and 0.2 m", "2 m/s and 2 m", "0.2 m/s and 2 m"], answer: 3, exp: "The bias integrates once into velocity, so velocity error = b times t = 0.01 x 20 = 0.2 m/s, and twice into position, so position error = one half b t squared = 0.5 x 0.01 x 400 = 2 m, which is why position diverges fastest. Option 0 is the tempting distractor because it gets the velocity right but drops the one-half factor and reports 4 m, while option 1 wrongly assumes both quantities grow linearly at b times t." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "A tracker takes five readings of a hand that is genuinely at x = 50 cm: 44, 46, 45, 47 and 43 cm. Using the foundations page's definitions accuracy = |E[x-hat] - x| and precision = Var(x-hat), what are the accuracy and the precision, and which quadrant is this?", opts: ["Accuracy 5 cm and precision 2 cm^2 - precise but inaccurate, the more dangerous quadrant", "Accuracy 5 cm and precision 10 cm^2 - precise but inaccurate, the more dangerous quadrant", "Accuracy 2 cm and precision 5 cm^2 - accurate but imprecise", "Accuracy 0 cm and precision 2 cm^2 - neither failure, because the five readings agree with one another"], answer: 0, exp: "Correct: the five readings sum to 225, so their mean is 225/5 = 45 cm and accuracy = |45 - 50| = 5 cm; the deviations from that mean are -1, +1, 0, +2 and -2, whose squares are 1, 1, 0, 4 and 4, so the variance - the mean of those squares - is 10/5 = 2 cm^2. Low variance with a constant 5 cm offset is exactly the precise-but-inaccurate quadrant the page calls the more dangerous one, because the readings agree with each other and therefore look trustworthy. Option 1 is the tempting distractor because 10 cm^2 is the sum of the squared deviations before it is divided by n, and option 3 mistakes agreement among the readings for correctness: the mean is 45 cm, not the true 50 cm, so the accuracy is 5 cm rather than 0." },

  /* Ch7 */
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "Week 7 distinguishes 'haptic feedback' from 'force feedback'. Which statement captures the distinction the deck draws?", opts: ["They are interchangeable: both mean feedback delivered through the skin.", "Haptic feedback means vibration, and force feedback means any vibration strong enough to feel like resistance.", "Haptic feedback is the broad category 'relating to the sense of touch'; force feedback is the narrower term for information produced mechanically for the kinaesthetic system.", "Force feedback is the broader term, and haptic feedback refers only to skin-surface pressure."], answer: 2, exp: "Haptic feedback is the widest term - anything relating to the sense of touch - while force feedback is defined by its target, the kinaesthetic system of muscles, tendons and joints. The tempting strong-vibration option fails because amplitude is not the criterion: a stronger buzz is still stimulation of cutaneous mechanoreceptors, which is exactly the 'common wrong answer' the foundations page flags." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "A game fits a wristband with an ERM vibration motor and asks it to signal 'the virtual sword is heavy'. The deck would call this a fundamental mismatch. Why?", opts: ["Weight is a kinaesthetic property felt through muscle tension and limb position, and a vibration motor stimulates cutaneous mechanoreceptors only.", "The ERM motor is simply too small; a larger eccentric mass would let the player feel the weight.", "The motor driver switches too slowly to encode a continuous property such as weight.", "Weight can only be signalled thermally, because heaviness is perceived as warmth in the hand."], answer: 0, exp: "Weight and resistance are carried by the kinaesthetic/proprioceptive channel, whereas vibration writes only to cutaneous mechanoreceptors, so the channel cannot carry the property regardless of amplitude. The bigger-motor option is the tempting misconception because it treats weight as a strength problem - a stronger buzz is still read as a stronger buzz, not as weight." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "The deck splits haptic research into an 'accuracy' camp and a 'naturalness' camp. Which pairing correctly describes what each side optimises?", opts: ["Accuracy-first favours passive props and retargeting; naturalness-first builds specialist controllers such as the CLAW.", "Both camps optimise the same thing - fidelity of a single object property - and differ only in where they publish.", "Accuracy-first optimises robustness and manufacturability; naturalness-first optimises the fidelity of a single object property.", "Accuracy-first builds specialist controllers that reproduce a specific object property with high fidelity (the CLAW, dynamic bar charts); naturalness-first seeks everyday-lifelike interaction using what the body and the room already provide, such as substitutional reality, passive props and retargeting."], answer: 3, exp: "The accuracy crowd is the CLAW and dynamic-bar-chart lineage that reproduces one property beautifully, while the naturalness crowd reaches for existing physical props and redirection rather than for more actuators. The reversed pairing is tempting because both camps sit in the same comparison table, but the deck's answer to the gap is to borrow robustness and generalizability from commercial design and the ideas from research - it never swaps what each camp optimises." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "The lecture builds the DC motor drive circuit one component at a time, each addition answering a problem the previous circuit could not. Which order matches that build-up?", opts: ["Battery + motor, then add the flyback diode, then add the transistor, then connect the grounds together.", "Battery + motor, then add the microcontroller and a transistor, then add the flyback diode, then connect the battery ground to the microcontroller ground.", "Battery + motor, then connect the grounds together, then add the transistor, then add the flyback diode.", "Battery + motor, then add the flyback diode, then connect the grounds together, then add the microcontroller and transistor."], answer: 1, exp: "The motor first spins with nothing controlling it, so the microcontroller and a transistor are added because the pin cannot drive the motor directly, then the flyback diode handles the current pushed back at power-down, and finally the two grounds are tied so the gate signal has a shared reference. Diode-before-transistor is the tempting ordering error, but the diode exists to protect the switch, so there is nothing to protect until the transistor is in the circuit." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "A VR demo lets the user touch one physical cup; in one scene it is a mug, in another a torch, in another a sword hilt. Which technique makes this possible, and how?", opts: ["Substitutional reality, because it replaces every physical element with a virtual counterpart.", "Electrical muscle stimulation, because the user's own muscles generate the force themselves.", "Haptic retargeting: the reach is redirected as it proceeds so one passive prop serves several virtual objects, with the real hand landing on the prop while the displayed hand lands on the virtual object.", "A grounded force-feedback arm, because one linkage can render the shape of any object."], answer: 2, exp: "Haptic retargeting is the dynamic repurposing of passive haptics credited to Azmandian et al., CHI '16: one prop, many virtual objects, achieved by redirecting the hand during the reach. Substitutional reality is the tempting option, but it is precisely the one-prop-per-virtual-object limitation being worked around, which is why it massively reduces the design opportunities." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "In the retargeting study's 2AFC procedure, participants report perceiving an offset on 50% of trials at a 14 degree rotation. What is the correct reading of that number?", opts: ["14 degrees is the detection threshold: with two alternatives the guessing rate is 0.5, so this is the point where performance leaves chance.", "14 degrees is a safe design margin, because half of the users still fail to notice the manipulation.", "14 degrees shows the user detects the offset perfectly, so it is the upper limit of what can be rendered.", "14 degrees is the s75 'clearly perceived' criterion applied to the offset curve."], answer: 0, exp: "In a two-alternative forced choice task the floor of the curve is the 0.5 guessing rate, not 0, so the 50% point is the detection threshold s50 (the foundations page's illustrative curve puts it around 14 to 15 degrees). The safe-margin option is the tempting misreading: half of your users noticing is a coin flip, which is why the study page says never to design at the 50% point, and the 75% point is the separate 'clearly perceived' criterion." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "The deck lists rhythm and ADSR alongside frequency and amplitude, and insists a haptic signal must change over time. What is the perceptual reason?", opts: ["The device gets weaker as it runs, so a signal that never changes simply fades below what the motor can produce.", "A constant drive current overheats the motor, so changing the signal protects the hardware.", "A steady vibration falls below the GPIO pin's current limit and stops being driven.", "Under a constant stimulus the percept adapts and fades, so it is the change in the envelope, not its absolute level, that keeps the signal felt."], answer: 3, exp: "Adaptation is how the response changes over time under a constant stimulus: a steady buzz fades from notice while the same vibration restarted a moment later is felt again, so rhythm, duration and ADSR keep it perceptible. The 'device gets weaker' option is the naming the foundations page flags as the common wrong answer - it is the percept that fades, not the motor's output, so a stronger or steadier drive does not restore it." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "Which statement about the families of force-producing haptics matches the deck's trade-off table?", opts: ["Electrical muscle stimulation gives the user more control, because the user's own muscles produce the force.", "Ultrasound focuses acoustic pressure in mid-air with nothing to hold, but the workspace is small and the forces are strong enough only for light tactile sensations.", "Grounded force feedback is portable and untethered, which is why it suits everyday wearable interfaces.", "Passive props remove the one-prop-per-object problem without needing any further technique."], answer: 1, exp: "The table pairs every approach with what it costs you, and for ultrasound the costs are a small workspace, weak forces and no grasp even though it needs no device to hold. The EMS option is the tempting reversal: because the electrodes drive your own muscles, the system is moving you rather than yielding to you, so what you lose is control rather than gain it." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "A haptic knob uses the foundations page's stepper motor: open loop, N = 200 steps per rotation, one pulse per step. How many pulses must the driver send to rotate the knob exactly 90 degrees?", opts: ["18 pulses", "90 pulses", "50 pulses", "200 pulses"], answer: 2, exp: "One step is 360/N = 360/200 = 1.8 degrees, so 90 degrees needs 90 / 1.8 = 50 pulses. 90 pulses is the tempting shortcut of one pulse per degree, which ignores the motor's fixed step angle; 200 pulses would be a full rotation, and 18 pulses would give only 32.4 degrees." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "A vibration motor draws 100 mA from its own 9 V supply and is switched by an NMOS transistor with R_DS(on) = 0.5 ohm. Using the foundations page's switch model, how much power does the transistor dissipate, and how does that compare with the motor?", opts: ["450 mW, because the switch and the motor share the load equally.", "900 mW, because the switch dissipates the same power as the motor.", "50 mW, from the product of current and on-resistance.", "5 mW, because P_sw = I^2 x R_DS(on) = (0.1 A)^2 x 0.5 ohm, against P_motor = 9 V x 0.1 A = 0.9 W, so the switch stays cool while the motor does the work."], answer: 3, exp: "P_sw = I_motor^2 x R_DS(on) = 0.01 x 0.5 = 0.005 W = 5 mW, while the motor's own consumption is P_motor = V_motor x I_motor = 9 x 0.1 = 0.9 W, so the MOSFET loses well under one percent of it. The tempting 50 mW comes from forgetting to square the current (0.1 x 0.5), and 900 mW is the motor's power rather than the switch's loss." },

  /* Ch8 */
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "The study guide contrasts two EU AI regulation definitions. Which statement is the current, behaviour-based wording rather than the earlier proposal's technique-based wording?", opts: ["Software developed with one or more of the techniques listed in Annex I, such as machine learning, symbolic reasoning, Bayesian estimation or search and optimisation", "A machine-based system designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy that may exhibit adaptiveness after deployment and that infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs that can influence physical or virtual environments", "A program that learns from data and improves its accuracy over time, whatever technique it happens to use", "Any system whose outputs could be produced by a human doing the same task"], answer: 1, exp: "The current wording is behaviour-based: it names no technique at all and instead asks whether the system infers outputs, with autonomy varying and adaptiveness possible after deployment. Option 0 is the earlier proposal, which is technique-based, and the guide says the two are not interchangeable; option 2 is the foundations page's named common wrong answer, because learning is one technique rather than the definition." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "A scheduling agent reads your calendar, books a meeting room, moves the meeting and only then sends you a notification. Where does it sit on Sheridan and Verplank's ten levels, and what is the main design risk?", opts: ["Level 5 - it asks the human to approve before executing, so the risk is confirmation fatigue", "Level 6 - it lets the human veto within a stated window, so the risk is a missed countdown", "Level 7 - it executes automatically and then necessarily informs, so the risk is an action that is hard to undo for other people", "Level 10 - it decides everything and ignores the human, so the risk is having no oversight at all"], answer: 2, exp: "Level 7 is exactly 'executes automatically, then necessarily informs the human', and the risk follows from reversibility: attendees have already been told, so the notification arrives too late. Level 5 is the tempting option but it requires approval before execution, and level 10 overstates the case because this system does inform you." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "The study guide's conversation-design procedure has seven steps. Which step comes immediately after 'decide the three-tiered confidence policy: act, confirm or ask again'?", opts: ["Design the repair paths - hyperarticulation, simplification, rephrasing, added detail", "Draft the happy-path dialogue as literal utterances, not as intents", "Enumerate the ways the same intent can be phrased, and the ways it can be misheard", "Cut whatever the transcript shows people never say"], answer: 0, exp: "The order runs: the job and who is speaking, the happy-path dialogue, the phrasing and mishearing variants, the confidence policy, the repair paths, testing with real people in the real setting including the noisy one, and only then cutting what the transcript shows people never say. Options 1 and 2 are earlier steps, and option 3 is the final step, so neither follows the confidence policy." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "A user types the valid UK postcode 'LA11GX' and the assistant rejects it with 'I need a valid zip code', never explaining its own format assumption. Which maxim does the study guide use this example to illustrate?", opts: ["Quality - make a contribution that is true", "Quantity - be as informative as required, and no more", "Relation - be relevant to what the user actually meant", "Manner - avoid obscurity and ambiguity, be brief and orderly, including about formats the system itself requires"], answer: 3, exp: "The deck's illustration of Manner is precisely an unexplained format assumption that rejects a valid postcode. Relation is the tempting distractor, but that maxim is the one broken by the assistant that answers 'what was the last song?' with a plot summary of the 2010 film The Last Song - a relevance failure, not a format one." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "A team pitches an automated essay grader. Using the study guide's three-way split of AI capability, what should you expect?", opts: ["Automating perception, so it will be highly accurate and the ethical concern is the scale of deployment", "Predicting social outcomes, which the deck calls fundamentally dubious and which should not be deployed", "Automating judgement - far from perfect but improving, so some error is inevitable and the design must handle it", "Automating judgement, where a high measured accuracy rate removes the ethical concern"], answer: 2, exp: "The deck places automated essay grading under automating judgement: imperfect but improving, with the ethical pressure coming from error that is inevitable and lands on a person. Option 1 is reserved for predicting social outcomes such as recidivism, and option 3 is wrong because some error survives any accuracy figure - the foundations page notes that an 85 % agreement rate quantifies error without removing it, and agreement is not accuracy." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "After a failed request, a user retries with 'Hey Google, play Hello by Adele' instead of 'play Hello'. Which repair strategy is this, and what does it tell the designer?", opts: ["Hyperarticulation - the acoustic model is failing, so the system should ask rather than retry the same parse", "Simplification - the user no longer believes the system can parse a sentence", "New utterance - the skill needs a synonym list rather than a louder prompt", "Giving more information - the first attempt was under-specified, so the system should have asked which one was meant"], answer: 3, exp: "Adding detail to disambiguate is the fourth documented pattern from Myers and colleagues (CHI '18), and it points at an under-specified first request rather than a hearing problem. Hyperarticulation is the tempting distractor, but it keeps the same words and changes the delivery - louder and slower - while 'new utterance' means reformulating the same goal with a different verb, as when 'modify my group meeting' becomes 'edit my group meeting'." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "The foundations page distinguishes over-trust from automation bias. Which pairing is correct?", opts: ["Over-trust is a personality trait of credulous users; automation bias is a property of the model's training data", "Over-trust is accepting a system's output as correct without checking it; automation bias is favouring the automated suggestion over other information, including the user's own judgement", "Over-trust is accepting output without checking it; automation bias is another name for a model that is biased against a group of people", "Over-trust is a system being over-confident in its own output; automation bias is a user who checks the output too often"], answer: 1, exp: "Those are the page's exact definitions, and both are made worse by confident-sounding output from an uncertain model. Option 2 gets over-trust right but repeats the named common wrong answer by using automation bias interchangeably with 'the model is biased', which is a different claim; option 0 wrongly makes over-trust a personality trait rather than a property of the interface." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "Eugene Goostman fooled 10 of 30 judges at the 2014 Royal Society competition, and the study guide also cites Moravec's paradox. What do the two cases together warn a designer against?", opts: ["Reading a benchmark or competition result as general competence - a carefully engineered persona can carry a limited system past human judges", "Building systems that answer every question, since a system that never admits ignorance must be generally intelligent", "Assuming perception and mobility are hard, when they are really the easy problems that adult-level reasoning solves", "Using human judges at all, since a Turing-test competition can never be run reproducibly"], answer: 0, exp: "The persona - a 13-year-old boy from Odessa who deliberately did not know everything, with a Ukrainian background that excused grammatical errors - was engineered to make failure look human, and Moravec's paradox warns against reading benchmark success as general competence. Option 2 is the tempting inversion: Moravec's paradox says perception and mobility are the difficult or impossible skills while adult-level test performance is comparatively easy." },
  { ch: 8, type: "math", q: "A voice assistant's pipeline measures wake word 250 ms, speech recognition 320 ms, language understanding 180 ms, dialogue management 90 ms, skill or API call 400 ms and text-to-speech 160 ms. Using L = L_wake + L_asr + L_nlu + L_dialogue + L_skill + L_tts, what is the round trip against a 1 000 ms budget?", opts: ["1 000 ms - exactly on budget, because the five on-device stages are the ones that count", "1 400 ms - 400 ms over budget", "1 240 ms - slightly over budget", "900 ms - under budget, because language understanding and dialogue management overlap"], answer: 1, exp: "The delays add rather than hide: 250 + 320 + 180 + 90 + 400 + 160 = 1 400 ms, which is 400 ms over the 1 000 ms budget. Option 0 is the tempting trap because the five on-device stages alone sum to exactly 1 000 ms, but the skill call is a stage of the same chain - it can be started speculatively from a partial transcript, yet it still counts in the measured sum." },
  { ch: 8, type: "math", q: "The foundations page models understanding as P(understood) = P(words) x P(syntax | words) x P(semantics | syntax) x P(pragmatics | semantics). If all four levels succeed with probability 0.9, what is P(understood) and what does the result show?", opts: ["0.90 - the chain is capped at its weakest level, so it cannot fall below 0.9", "0.60 - the four 10 % failure rates add up, so understanding is 1 - 0.4", "3.60 - the four stage probabilities sum along the chain", "About 0.66 - the terms multiply (0.9^4 = 0.6561), so a stage at 0.9 caps the whole chain"], answer: 3, exp: "Because each level conditions on the previous one the probabilities multiply: 0.9 x 0.9 x 0.9 x 0.9 = 0.6561, about 0.66. Option 0 half-remembers the page's phrase that a stage at 0.9 caps the chain but mistakes the cap for equality, since the product is strictly below the weakest stage; option 1 wrongly adds the error terms instead of multiplying the success terms." },
];
